From: Kettle Linux
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: sm8750: keep the ADSP remote heap out of Linux

The ADSP's fastrpc remote heap is a reusable shared-dma-pool, so its
12 MiB stay in the page allocator as CMA. But fastrpc hands the whole
region to the LPASS and ADSP heap VMs at probe (qcom,vmids ->
fastrpc_init_reserved_mem() -> qcom_scm_assign_mem()), without
allocating it from the pool first. Linux loses access to memory its own
allocator still gives out.

Under memory pressure, movable allocations (page cache) land in the
pool, and the first touch of such a page faults. On the AYN Odin 3
Portal a large copy (tar of /home from the SD card to UFS) brought
that on every time free memory ran out, about 2:45 into the copy. Most
times the whole SoC went down (a PMIC fault reset, sometimes powered
off, no kernel message). Once it survived and logged "synchronous
external abort" in clear_page/copy_page/__arch_copy_from_user, with
every faulting address in 0xf2c00000-0xf37fffff, the heap.

Make the region no-map, as hamoa, lemans and monaco describe theirs:
it is never Linux's memory. It costs 12 MiB of RAM. Tested on the Odin 3
Portal with the dtb alone: the same copy loop held (see
docs/ODIN3PORTAL.md).
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8750.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8750.dtsi
@@ -570,7 +570,7 @@
 			alloc-ranges = <0x0 0x00000000 0x0 0xffffffff>;
 			alignment = <0x0 0x400000>;
 			size = <0x0 0xc00000>;
-			reusable;
+			no-map;
 		};
 	};

